\section{Compute in Logical Instants}

Lustre nodes are functions from input streams to output streams, defined by equations. Pointwise operators act within one logical instant. The initialized delay \texttt{fby}, or equivalently initialization with \texttt{->} plus \texttt{pre} in common Lustre syntax, carries information between instants. A causal schedule may order same-instant equations, but delayed dependencies read memory from the preceding instant and do not form combinational cycles \cite{halbwachs1991lustre}.

A clock denotes the instants at which a stream is present. Sampling with \texttt{when} creates a subclock; \texttt{merge} combines complementary subclocked values. Absence is not an arbitrary default value. Clock calculus rejects equations that combine values whose presences do not agree.

\section{Write an Initialized Counter}

This Lustre node resets a running sum:

\begin{verbatim}
node counter(ini, inc: int; res: bool) returns (n: int);
let
  n = if (true -> false) or res
      then ini
      else (0 -> pre n) + inc;
tel
\end{verbatim}

At the first instant, \texttt{true -> false} selects \texttt{ini}; later, \texttt{res} does so. The delayed \texttt{n} is initialized by \(0\), so no uninitialized value reaches the output. \Cref{fig:visual-362} separates the memory read from its end-of-reaction update.

